
		asc (Adaptive Skeleton Climbing) ver 2.0a
		=========================================
				4 Oct 2001


The alpha of the second version of asc package. If there is any problem or
question, please email me at ttwong@acm.org

This package contains the C++ implementation of the "Adaptive Skeleton
Climbing" multiresolution isosurface extraction technique. 


HOW TO INSTALL 
==============

This software have been tested on SUN Solaris (or UNIX platforms) and
Windows . If you only needed the isosurface extraction program, this is what
you need.
	
	Solaris: GNU g++ and gcc compiler 
	Windows: Visual C++ 6.0 or above

If you need the OpenGL viewer of triangular mesh, you need to have the
following:

	Solaris: Mesa
	Windows: OpenGL


SOLARIS:
--------
To compile it on Solaris, rename Makefile.solaris to Makefile. 
Edit "Makefile" to make sure the paths are correctly set.
Three paths: MESAPATH, X11PATH and GLUTPATH needed to be set.
To compile, type "make"

To compile it on Windows, click "asc.dsw" or "asc.dsp" to start 
the project file. Click "Build->Batch Build" to build asc1 to asc8.

Four version of asc will be compiled by default: asc1, asc2, asc4, asc8
All have the name in the form of asc[n], where (n+1)x(n+1)x(n+1) is the
size of block. asc1 will produce the finest triangular mesh (bascially
same as marching cube). asc8 will produce the coarest mesh among the 4 
programs.

Program "viewtri" is an OpenGL based program to view the extracted 
triangular mesh.

All generated executable programs are stored in subdirectory bin/ of
the asc package.


Install the asc libraries:

You can also create the asc libraries and link with your program.
However, due to current implementation, asc routines manipulate on
block with different size are archived in different library files.
That is, routines on block with size 4 is archived in library "libasc4.a"
Routines on block with size 8 is archived in library "libasc8.a"
All libraries are named in the form of "libasc[n].a", where n is the block
size.  To make the libraries, type "make library". All generated library
files are located in subdirectory lib/ of the package.



MS Windows
----------
You are assumed to be equipped with Visual C++ 6.0 or above. For other
compiler, you may need to create the project file yourself.

To compile all (including ascX, ascX.lib, library testing programs and
viewtri):
- Start the file "asc.dsw"
- Click on the menu bar
  Build -> Batch Build
- Make sure all configurations have been "ticked". Press "Rebuild All"
  button to build all.
- Then all asc programs, libraries, library testing programs and viewtri
  will be built. To successfully build viewtri (the OpenGL triangular mesh
  viewer), you need to have the opengl32.lib, glu32.lib and glut32.lib 
  libraries already installed on your PC.




HOW TO USE
==========

Usage:      asc? [-t <threshold>] [-d? [<voxelfile>]] [other options] > outfile
       or   asc? [-t <threshold>] [-d? [<voxelfile>]] -f <outfile> [other options]

       asc? can be [asc1, asc2, asc4, asc8]
       -d?  is one of [-ds, -dk, -dh, -dm, -dr1, -dr2], see below

Options:
  -a <angle thresh>	This option control the quality of the generated
			triangular mesh. Make sure the generated mesh
			match the gradient in the voxel grid. Larger
			angle thresh means larger tolerance. Hence
			poorer quality.
			Set the initial angular threshold in degree
			(default: 15 degree)

  -c+   		communcate among blocks (default), hence no gap.

  -c-   		do not communicate, gap may appears.

  -ds   		init data with 2 metasphere (default)

  -dk   		init data with knot surface

  -dh <pgm file>   	init data with heightfield file in PGM

  -dm <mirafile>   	specify the MIRA file to read as data

  -dr1 <rawfile> <w> <d> <h> specify 8-bit raw byte file with w x d x h

  -dr2 <rawfile> <w> <d> <h> specify 16-bit raw file with w x d x h

  -f <outfile>          specify output filename, default is stdout.

  -ha+  		Enable the flag to handle ambiguity (default)

  -ha-  		Disable the function of handling ambiguity.

  -hb+  		Enable the flag handle beauty of triangles (default).

  -hb-  		Do not handle beauty

  -i <idxfile> 		Provide a kdtree indexed file for fast searching
			This function is currently disabled.

  -ob   		output triangle binary (see below for the 
		        propietrary format, default output format)

  -or   		output triangle mesh in RenderMan ascii rib format.

  -ot                   output triangle mesh in TPoly format

  -n+   		output with normal info (default)

  -n-   		output no normal info

  -r <res>   		if voxel data are generated by the program itself,
			the resolution of the volume data can be specified
			by this option. Default resolution is 128x128x128.

  -t <threshold>   	set the threshold value

Even you don't provide any option, the program can still generate its
own voxel data and using default threshold value to extract isosurface
and output in default file format.

Default: Generate metaball voxel data,
	 Threshold value is 100,
	 Output triangular mesh in Triangle Binary format. 

Examples:
1) Given a MIRA voxel data file and threshold value, generate the triangular
   mesh. Use the block size of 8

   asc8  -dm cthead.mira  -t 150 > cthead.tri

2) Tell the program to generate "knot" voxel data with resolution of 
   256x256x256 for isosurface extraction.
   Threshold value is set to 200. Output to file "knot.tri". Using block
   size of 4.

   asc4  -dk  -r 256  -t 200 -f knot.tri



FORMAT OF TRIANGLE BINARY
=========================
The format of the default output triangle binary format is very simple.
Simply,

 <V1x, V1y, V1z> <N1x, N1y, N1z> \
 <V2x, V2y, V2z> <N2x, N2y, N2z>  +- information of the first triangle
 <V3x, V3y, V3z> <N3x, N3y, N3z> /

 <V1x, V1y, V1z> <N1x, N1y, N1z> \
 <V2x, V2y, V2z> <N2x, N2y, N2z>  +- information of the second triangle
 <V3x, V3y, V3z> <N3x, N3y, N3z> /

 ....

 V1x is the x coordinate of vertex 1
 N1x is the x coordinate of surface normal at vertex 1
 All values are in "float" in standard C language.
 There is no header.



HOW TO LINK ASC LIBRARY
=======================

1) First include the header file "interface.h" in subdirectory include/

2) Call any one of the following routines to generate isosurfaces.

void asc1(volume *v, float threshold, char *outfile);
void asc2(volume *v, float threshold, char *outfile);
void asc4(volume *v, float threshold, char *outfile);
void asc8(volume *v, float threshold, char *outfile);

v is the input voxel data structure
threshold is the threshold value
outfile is the output file name, in Triangle Binary format.

3) The input data structure volume is described in the header file vortex.h

4) see example program "testlib.cpp".




HOW TO USE viewtri
==================
An OpenGL-based triangular meshes viewer is included in the asc package.
Here is the usage of the viewer.

  Usage:  viewtri -ib <meshfile> [other options]

  Options:
    -ib <meshfile>  input Triangle binary

    -ir <meshfile>  input Rib

    -it <meshfile>  input TPoly

    -f              flip the normal of all input triangles. 
		    Sometimes the normal is pointed to opposite direction.

    	            You can explicitly scale the mesh by
    -x <scale>	    explicity scale x

    -y <scale>      explicity scale y

    -z <scale>      explicity scale z

When the program is invoked, you can pressed the right mouse button
to trigger the popup menu. You may observe some gap between the adjacent
triangles when rotating the mesh. It is because the viewer's backface
culling flag is turned on by default. You can turn it off using the
popup menu.


EXAMPLE
=======
The best way to teach you to use the package is by trying some examples.
You can actually try them since all data file are provided in the 
subdirectory examples/

Example 1
---------
For example, you have a voxel data file called "knot.mira" in MIRA format.
You want a coarse mesh. You can extract the isosurface given the threshold
value 200 by 

  csh> asc8 -t 200 -dm knot.mira -f knot.bin

Now the isosurface is extracted and stored in the Triangle Binary file
called "knot.bin". To view the mesh in a system with OpenGL library, type

  csh> viewtri -ib knot.bin -f

By default the surface normal associated with the triangle vertices are
inverted. You have to invert them for display by adding the option "-f"
Now you can rotate the mesh by dragging the screen with your mouse.





